---
title: Modal
description: Install the Modal provider, set a token pair and an app name, and create a sandbox from a container image.
---

Modal sandboxes start from a container image. You need a Modal token pair and an app that already exists. This release talks to Modal SDK v0.11.1.

## Install

<Tabs groupId="language" items={['TypeScript', 'Go']} persist>
  <Tab value="TypeScript">

Node.js 20, 22, or 24.15+.

```sh
npm install @sandbox-kit/sandbox @sandbox-kit/modal
```

  </Tab>
  <Tab value="Go">

Go 1.26.0 or later.

```sh
go get github.com/sandbox-kit/kit/sdks/go/sandbox@latest
go get github.com/sandbox-kit/kit/sdks/go/providers/modal@latest
```

  </Tab>
</Tabs>

## Create a sandbox

Set `MODAL_TOKEN_ID`, `MODAL_TOKEN_SECRET`, and `MODAL_APP_NAME`. `MODAL_ENVIRONMENT` defaults to `main` when you omit it.

<Tabs groupId="language" items={['TypeScript', 'Go']} persist>
  <Tab value="TypeScript">

```ts
import { modal } from "@sandbox-kit/modal";
import { Client } from "@sandbox-kit/sandbox";

await using client = new Client({
  provider: modal(),
  auth: {
    tokenPair: {
      id: process.env.MODAL_TOKEN_ID!,
      secret: process.env.MODAL_TOKEN_SECRET!,
    },
  },
  scope: {
    appName: process.env.MODAL_APP_NAME!,
    environment: process.env.MODAL_ENVIRONMENT ?? "main",
  },
  timeout: 120_000,
});

const sandbox = await client.create({
  source: { image: { reference: "alpine:3.21" } },
  resources: { cpuCores: 0.5, memoryMib: 512n },
  lifetime: { maximumLifetime: 5 * 60_000 },
});

console.log(sandbox.id);
```

`timeout` and `maximumLifetime` are milliseconds. `memoryMib` is a bigint. `await using` closes the client when the block ends. Call `await client.close()` in a `finally` block when that syntax is unavailable.

  </Tab>
  <Tab value="Go">

```go
package main

import (
	"context"
	"fmt"
	"os"
	"time"

	"github.com/sandbox-kit/kit/sdks/go/providers/modal"
	"github.com/sandbox-kit/kit/sdks/go/sandbox"
)

func main() {
	client, err := sandbox.NewClient(modal.New(), &sandbox.Config{
		Auth: &sandbox.AuthConfig{Credentials: &sandbox.TokenPair{
			TokenPair: &sandbox.TokenPairCredentials{
				Id:     os.Getenv("MODAL_TOKEN_ID"),
				Secret: os.Getenv("MODAL_TOKEN_SECRET"),
			},
		}},
		Scope: &sandbox.Scope{
			AppName:     sandbox.Value(os.Getenv("MODAL_APP_NAME")),
			Environment: sandbox.Value("main"),
		},
		Timeout: sandbox.Duration(2 * time.Minute),
	})
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
	defer client.Close(context.Background())

	instance, err := client.Create(context.Background(), &sandbox.CreateOptions{
		Source: &sandbox.SandboxSource{Kind: &sandbox.Image{
			Image: &sandbox.ImageSource{Reference: "alpine:3.21"},
		}},
		Resources: &sandbox.Resources{
			CpuCores:  sandbox.Value(0.5),
			MemoryMib: sandbox.Value(uint64(512)),
		},
		Lifetime: &sandbox.LifetimePolicy{
			MaximumLifetime: sandbox.Duration(5 * time.Minute),
		},
	})
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
	fmt.Println(instance.ID())
}
```

  </Tab>
</Tabs>

Omit `auth` when the Modal SDK should use credentials it already finds in the environment or a Modal profile. `scope.appName` is still required.

`endpoint` is not a Modal setting. The client rejects it before it calls Modal.

## What you can do next

Set the command, working directory, CPU, memory, region, ports, and an idle timeout on the [create](/docs/create) page.

Modal does not support start, stop, pause, archive, or delete. To finish a sandbox, [attach and terminate](/docs/lifecycle) it. A maximum lifetime or an idle timeout also ends it.

When you set resources, Modal requires at least 0.125 CPU cores (steps of 0.001) and 128 MiB of memory. Leave a resource out to keep Modal's default. Disk size cannot be raised at create time.

Runnable project: [create-modal-sandbox](https://github.com/sandbox-kit/kit/tree/main/examples/typescript/modal/create-modal-sandbox) (TypeScript) and [the Go project](https://github.com/sandbox-kit/kit/tree/main/examples/go/modal/create-modal-sandbox).

---
title: Manage a sandbox
description: Look up a sandbox, stop or terminate it, and close your local handle without shutting the sandbox down.
---

Create returns a handle. Later calls use that handle, or a handle you open from an id. Closing the client or the handle does not shut the sandbox down.

| You want to | Daytona | Modal |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Open an existing sandbox | `get` checks the id with Daytona | `attach` takes the id and does not look it up |
| Start, stop, pause, archive, delete | Supported | Unsupported |
| Finish the sandbox | `delete`, or a lifetime policy | `terminate`, or a lifetime policy |
| Drop your local connection | `close` on the client | `close` on the client |

`supports` tells you whether this provider implements the call. A missing operation fails before the provider is contacted. Daytona can still reject a supported call for one sandbox, and that error comes back to you.

<Tabs groupId="provider" items={['Daytona', 'Modal']} persist>
  <Tab value="Daytona">

`get` loads a sandbox that already exists. `stop` asks Daytona to stop it. The sandbox stays available to start again. `delete` removes it.

<Tabs groupId="language" items={['TypeScript', 'Go']} persist>
  <Tab value="TypeScript">

```ts
const sandbox = await client.get(process.env.DAYTONA_SANDBOX_ID!);

if (!sandbox.supports(LifecycleCapability.Stop)) {
  throw new Error(`sandbox ${sandbox.id} cannot be stopped`);
}

const info = await sandbox.stop({ timeout: 60_000 });
console.log(sandbox.id, info.state);
```

`timeout` is milliseconds. Import `LifecycleCapability` from `@sandbox-kit/sandbox`.

To remove it:

```ts
await sandbox.delete({ timeout: 60_000 });
```

  </Tab>
  <Tab value="Go">

```go
instance, err := client.Get(ctx, os.Getenv("DAYTONA_SANDBOX_ID"))
if err != nil {
	return err
}
if !instance.Supports(sandbox.LifecycleCapabilityStop) {
	return fmt.Errorf("sandbox %s cannot be stopped", instance.ID())
}
if err := instance.Stop(ctx, &sandbox.StopOptions{
	Timeout: sandbox.Duration(time.Minute),
}); err != nil {
	return err
}
```

`Delete` takes `DeleteOptions` the same way. `Get` checks the id with Daytona. `Attach` is not available.

  </Tab>
</Tabs>

`pause` keeps memory and stops the sandbox. `archive` moves a retained sandbox into archive storage. `start` brings a stopped or archived sandbox back. Check `supports` before you call them. Daytona decides whether that sandbox can pause, archive, or stop.

`sandbox.close()` does not contact Daytona. You can keep using the handle.

  </Tab>
  <Tab value="Modal">

`attach` builds a handle from an id. It does not ask Modal whether that sandbox exists. `terminate` finishes the sandbox. It is not a stop, and it is not a delete.

<Tabs groupId="language" items={['TypeScript', 'Go']} persist>
  <Tab value="TypeScript">

```ts
const sandbox = await client.attach(process.env.MODAL_SANDBOX_ID!);

if (!sandbox.supports(LifecycleCapability.Terminate)) {
  throw new Error(`sandbox ${sandbox.id} cannot be terminated`);
}

const exit = await sandbox.terminate({ wait: true, timeout: 60_000 });
console.log(sandbox.id, exit.exitCode);
```

`wait: true` polls until the sandbox exits or the timeout fires. The timeout is how long the client waits. Modal does not receive that duration as its own terminate timeout.

  </Tab>
  <Tab value="Go">

```go
instance, err := client.Attach(ctx, os.Getenv("MODAL_SANDBOX_ID"))
if err != nil {
	return err
}
if !instance.Supports(sandbox.LifecycleCapabilityTerminate) {
	return fmt.Errorf("sandbox %s cannot be terminated", instance.ID())
}
exit, err := instance.Terminate(ctx, &sandbox.TerminateOptions{
	Wait:    sandbox.Value(true),
	Timeout: sandbox.Duration(time.Minute),
})
if err != nil {
	return err
}
fmt.Println(exit.GetExitCode())
```

  </Tab>
</Tabs>

`get`, `start`, `stop`, `pause`, `archive`, and `delete` are rejected for Modal. Use a maximum lifetime or an idle timeout at [create](/docs/create) time when you want the sandbox to end on its own.

`sandbox.close()` does not terminate the sandbox. You can keep using the handle.

  </Tab>
</Tabs>

## Close

<Tabs groupId="language" items={['TypeScript', 'Go']} persist>
  <Tab value="TypeScript">

```ts
await client.close();
```

Prefer `await using` when the client's lifetime matches the block. Disposing the client closes the connection. Disposing the handle does not stop or terminate the sandbox.

  </Tab>
  <Tab value="Go">

```go
defer client.Close(context.Background())
```

`Sandbox.Close` does not change the sandbox. A later call on that handle still reaches the provider.

  </Tab>
</Tabs>

`info.state` is the portable state. The provider's own string is kept beside it. After a lifecycle call, read the handle again before you decide the sandbox is stopped or gone. A failed call can mean the provider already changed it.

---
title: Read an error
description: Branch on the error kind. The same kinds are returned for Daytona and Modal.
---

A failed call throws in TypeScript and returns an error in Go. Both carry a kind, the provider, the operation, and, when a setting was rejected, the field that failed. Branch on the kind. The same list applies to Daytona and Modal.

The kind names a category. It does not mean the call is safe to retry. Sandbox Kit does not retry for you.

<Tabs groupId="language" items={['TypeScript', 'Go']} persist>
  <Tab value="TypeScript">

```ts
import { ErrorKind, SandboxError } from "@sandbox-kit/sandbox";

try {
  await client.create(request);
} catch (error) {
  if (!(error instanceof SandboxError)) throw error;

  switch (error.kind) {
    case ErrorKind.InvalidArgument:
    case ErrorKind.Unsupported:
      console.error("Check configuration:", error.field, error.message);
      break;
    case ErrorKind.Authentication:
      console.error("Check your provider credentials.");
      break;
    case ErrorKind.PermissionDenied:
      console.error("Your account does not have permission for this operation.");
      break;
    case ErrorKind.NotFound:
      console.error("The requested sandbox was not found.");
      break;
    case ErrorKind.RateLimited:
    case ErrorKind.ResourceExhausted:
      console.error("The provider's rate or resource limit was reached.");
      break;
    case ErrorKind.Canceled:
      console.error("The operation was canceled.");
      break;
    case ErrorKind.Timeout:
      console.error("The operation timed out.");
      break;
    default:
      console.error(error);
  }
}
```

The provider's own error is `error.cause`. Compare `error.field` with the field constants exported from `@sandbox-kit/sandbox` when you need one setting, such as CPU.

  </Tab>
  <Tab value="Go">

```go
var detail *sandbox.Error
if errors.As(err, &detail) {
	switch detail.Kind {
	case sandbox.ErrorKindInvalidArgument, sandbox.ErrorKindUnsupported:
		fmt.Println("Check configuration:", detail.Field)
	case sandbox.ErrorKindAuthentication:
		fmt.Println("Check your provider credentials.")
	case sandbox.ErrorKindPermissionDenied:
		fmt.Println("Your account does not have permission for this operation.")
	case sandbox.ErrorKindNotFound:
		fmt.Println("The requested sandbox was not found.")
	case sandbox.ErrorKindRateLimited, sandbox.ErrorKindResourceExhausted:
		fmt.Println("The provider's rate or resource limit was reached.")
	case sandbox.ErrorKindCanceled:
		fmt.Println("The operation was canceled.")
	case sandbox.ErrorKindTimeout:
		fmt.Println("The operation timed out.")
	default:
		fmt.Println(err)
	}
}
```

`Unwrap` returns the provider error, so `errors.Is(err, context.Canceled)` still matches. You can branch on `Kind` without importing the provider's error package.

  </Tab>
</Tabs>

Go and TypeScript use the same field string. A rule about one setting names that setting, such as `resources.cpu_cores`. A rule about several settings names their parent, such as `network`.

## After a failure

An error does not prove that no sandbox exists. The provider may have created one before a later step failed, and the error does not include that id. A lifecycle call can fail after the provider has already changed the sandbox. Look it up with `get` on Daytona before you retry. Modal `attach` does not perform that lookup.

Runnable projects: [Daytona errors](https://github.com/sandbox-kit/kit/tree/main/examples/typescript/daytona/handle-daytona-errors) and [Modal errors](https://github.com/sandbox-kit/kit/tree/main/examples/typescript/modal/handle-modal-errors). The Go projects sit beside them under `examples/go/`.

