---
title: Modal
description: Install the Modal provider, set a token pair and an app name, and create a sandbox from an image.
---

A Modal sandbox starts from a container image. You need a Modal token pair and an app that already exists. This client talks to Modal SDK v0.11.1.

## Install

<Tabs groupId="language" items={['TypeScript', 'Go']} persist>
  <Tab value="TypeScript">

```sh
npm install @sandbox-kit/sandbox @sandbox-kit/modal
```

  </Tab>
  <Tab value="Go">

```sh
go get github.com/sandbox-kit/kit/sdks/go/sandbox@latest
go get github.com/sandbox-kit/kit/sdks/go/providers/modal@latest
```

  </Tab>
</Tabs>

## Create a sandbox

Set `MODAL_TOKEN_ID`, `MODAL_TOKEN_SECRET`, and `MODAL_APP_NAME`. This program uses the environment `main` when `MODAL_ENVIRONMENT` is unset.

<Tabs groupId="language" items={['TypeScript', 'Go']} persist>
  <Tab value="TypeScript">

```ts
import { modal } from "@sandbox-kit/modal";
import { Client } from "@sandbox-kit/sandbox";

await using client = new Client({
  provider: modal(),
  auth: {
    tokenPair: {
      id: process.env.MODAL_TOKEN_ID!,
      secret: process.env.MODAL_TOKEN_SECRET!,
    },
  },
  scope: {
    appName: process.env.MODAL_APP_NAME!,
    environment: process.env.MODAL_ENVIRONMENT ?? "main",
  },
  timeout: 120_000,
});

const sandbox = await client.create({
  source: { image: { reference: "alpine:3.21" } },
  resources: { cpuCores: 0.5, memoryMib: 512n },
  lifetime: { maximumLifetime: 5 * 60_000 },
});

console.log(sandbox.id);
```

`timeout` and `maximumLifetime` are milliseconds. `memoryMib` is a bigint. `await using` closes the client when the block ends. Call `await client.close()` in a `finally` block when that syntax is unavailable.

  </Tab>
  <Tab value="Go">

```go
package main

import (
	"context"
	"fmt"
	"os"
	"time"

	"github.com/sandbox-kit/kit/sdks/go/providers/modal"
	"github.com/sandbox-kit/kit/sdks/go/sandbox"
)

func main() {
	client, err := sandbox.NewClient(modal.New(), &sandbox.Config{
		Auth: &sandbox.AuthConfig{Credentials: &sandbox.TokenPair{
			TokenPair: &sandbox.TokenPairCredentials{
				Id:     os.Getenv("MODAL_TOKEN_ID"),
				Secret: os.Getenv("MODAL_TOKEN_SECRET"),
			},
		}},
		Scope: &sandbox.Scope{
			AppName:     sandbox.Value(os.Getenv("MODAL_APP_NAME")),
			Environment: sandbox.Value("main"),
		},
		Timeout: sandbox.Duration(2 * time.Minute),
	})
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
	defer client.Close(context.Background())

	instance, err := client.Create(context.Background(), &sandbox.CreateOptions{
		Source: &sandbox.SandboxSource{Kind: &sandbox.Image{
			Image: &sandbox.ImageSource{Reference: "alpine:3.21"},
		}},
		Resources: &sandbox.Resources{
			CpuCores:  sandbox.Value(0.5),
			MemoryMib: sandbox.Value(uint64(512)),
		},
		Lifetime: &sandbox.LifetimePolicy{
			MaximumLifetime: sandbox.Duration(5 * time.Minute),
		},
	})
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
	fmt.Println(instance.ID())
}
```

  </Tab>
</Tabs>

Omit `auth` when the Modal SDK should use credentials it already finds in the environment or a Modal profile. `scope.appName` stays required. `endpoint` is rejected. Closing the client does not terminate the sandbox.

## Where to go next

| You want to | Open |
| --- | --- |
| A command, a region, ports, or an idle timeout | [Create a sandbox](/docs/create#modal) |
| Attach to a sandbox and terminate it | [Manage a sandbox](/docs/lifecycle#modal) |
| A failed call | [Read an error](/docs/errors) |
| A project you can run | [create-modal-sandbox](https://github.com/sandbox-kit/kit/tree/main/examples/typescript/modal/create-modal-sandbox) and the [Go project](https://github.com/sandbox-kit/kit/tree/main/examples/go/modal/create-modal-sandbox) |

---
title: Create a sandbox
description: Choose an image or a snapshot, set a size, and decide how long the sandbox lives.
---

`client.create` in TypeScript and `Client.Create` in Go return a handle. The handle's id is the sandbox you created. Construct the client on your provider page, or on [Configure the client](/docs/configuration).

Leave a field out to keep the provider's default. The client rejects a field that provider does not support, before it makes the call. Branch on that failure in [Read an error](/docs/errors).

TypeScript durations on this request are milliseconds. A missing property means the field was omitted. Go uses `sandbox.Value` for an optional number, including zero, and `sandbox.Duration` for a timeout or a delay.

## Units

| | Daytona | Modal |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Source | Omit for the default snapshot, or pass a snapshot or an image | Registry image, required |
| CPU | Whole cores | Fractional cores, minimum 0.125, step 0.001 |
| Memory | Whole GiB, written as MiB (`4096` is 4 GiB) | MiB, minimum 128 when set |
| Disk | Whole GiB, written as MiB | Cannot be set |
| Lifetime | TTL, and stop, pause, archive, or delete policies | Maximum lifetime and idle termination |
| Placement | `region` on the client. A placement block on this request is rejected | `placement` on this request. Client `region` is the default |

Account quotas, image names, regions, and capacity are checked by the provider.

## Daytona

A size override requires an image. A snapshot create keeps the snapshot's size.

<Tabs groupId="language" items={['TypeScript', 'Go']} persist>
  <Tab value="TypeScript">

```ts
const sandbox = await client.create({
  source: { image: { reference: "daytonaio/playground:latest" } },
  resources: {
    cpuCores: 2,
    memoryMib: 4096n,
    diskMib: 8192n,
  },
});
```

  </Tab>
  <Tab value="Go">

```go
instance, err := client.Create(ctx, &sandbox.CreateOptions{
	Source: &sandbox.SandboxSource{Kind: &sandbox.Image{
		Image: &sandbox.ImageSource{Reference: "daytonaio/playground:latest"},
	}},
	Resources: &sandbox.Resources{
		CpuCores:  sandbox.Value(2.0),
		MemoryMib: sandbox.Value(uint64(4096)),
		DiskMib:   sandbox.Value(uint64(8192)),
	},
})
```

  </Tab>
</Tabs>

Name a snapshot with `source.snapshot.reference`, for example `daytona-small`. Set the toolbox with `runtime.language` and `runtime.user`.

A linked sandbox must be ephemeral: `network.linkedSandbox` together with `lifetime.ephemeral: true`. The client rejects the request otherwise.

Idle stop, idle pause, stopped archive, and stopped delete use `PolicyMode` from `@sandbox-kit/sandbox` (`sandbox.PolicyModeAfter` and `sandbox.PolicyModeDisabled` in Go). A positive stop delay and a positive pause delay cannot be combined. Projects for [snapshots](https://github.com/sandbox-kit/kit/tree/main/examples/typescript/daytona/create-daytona-from-snapshot), [policies](https://github.com/sandbox-kit/kit/tree/main/examples/typescript/daytona/create-daytona-with-policies), [labels](https://github.com/sandbox-kit/kit/tree/main/examples/typescript/daytona/create-daytona-with-labels), and [network](https://github.com/sandbox-kit/kit/tree/main/examples/typescript/daytona/create-daytona-with-network) are in the repository. Go projects sit beside them under `examples/go/daytona/`.

## Modal

Pass a registry image. The app name belongs on the client, not on this request. Leave a resource out to keep Modal's default.

<Tabs groupId="language" items={['TypeScript', 'Go']} persist>
  <Tab value="TypeScript">

```ts
import { PolicyMode } from "@sandbox-kit/sandbox";

const sandbox = await client.create({
  source: { image: { reference: "alpine:3.21" } },
  resources: { cpuCores: 0.5, memoryMib: 512n },
  runtime: {
    workingDirectory: "/tmp",
    entrypoint: ["sleep", "infinity"],
  },
  lifetime: {
    maximumLifetime: 5 * 60_000,
    idleTerminate: { mode: PolicyMode.After, after: 60_000 },
  },
});
```

`PolicyMode.After` starts the idle timer. `PolicyMode.Disabled` turns idle termination off.

  </Tab>
  <Tab value="Go">

```go
instance, err := client.Create(ctx, &sandbox.CreateOptions{
	Source: &sandbox.SandboxSource{Kind: &sandbox.Image{
		Image: &sandbox.ImageSource{Reference: "alpine:3.21"},
	}},
	Resources: &sandbox.Resources{
		CpuCores:  sandbox.Value(0.5),
		MemoryMib: sandbox.Value(uint64(512)),
	},
	Runtime: &sandbox.Runtime{
		WorkingDirectory: sandbox.Value("/tmp"),
		Entrypoint:       []string{"sleep", "infinity"},
	},
	Lifetime: &sandbox.LifetimePolicy{
		MaximumLifetime: sandbox.Duration(5 * time.Minute),
		IdleTerminate: &sandbox.AutomaticAction{
			Mode:  sandbox.PolicyModeAfter,
			After: sandbox.Duration(time.Minute),
		},
	},
})
```

  </Tab>
</Tabs>

Set cloud and region on `placement`, for example `{ cloud: "auto", regions: ["us-east-1"] }`. A ready wait needs a probe and a positive timeout.

Modal rejects a disk size, an API endpoint, and Daytona stop or archive policies. Projects for [resources](https://github.com/sandbox-kit/kit/tree/main/examples/typescript/modal/create-modal-with-resources), [idle timeout](https://github.com/sandbox-kit/kit/tree/main/examples/typescript/modal/create-modal-idle), [placement](https://github.com/sandbox-kit/kit/tree/main/examples/typescript/modal/create-modal-with-placement), and [readiness](https://github.com/sandbox-kit/kit/tree/main/examples/typescript/modal/create-modal-ready) are in the repository. Go projects sit beside them under `examples/go/modal/`.

## What you get back

`info()` in TypeScript and `Info()` in Go return a copy of the metadata. Editing that copy does not change the sandbox. Reported CPU and memory can differ from the numbers you sent.

This client creates the sandbox and manages its lifetime. Commands inside the sandbox, GPU requests, resizing, extra storage, and browsers are outside it.

---
title: Manage a sandbox
description: Open a sandbox you already have, stop or terminate it, and close your local connection.
---

Create returns a handle. Later calls use that handle, or a handle you open from an id. Pick the row for the call you need.

| You want to | Daytona | Modal |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Open an existing sandbox | [`get`](#open-a-sandbox) looks the id up | [`attach`](#open-a-sandbox) takes the id and does not look it up |
| Start, stop, pause, archive | [Supported](#stop-start-pause-and-archive) | Rejected before the call |
| Remove the sandbox | [`delete`](#delete) | Use [`terminate`](#terminate), or a lifetime set at create time |
| Drop your local connection | [`close`](#close-the-client) | [`close`](#close-the-client) |

`supports` reports whether this provider implements the call. Import `LifecycleCapability` from `@sandbox-kit/sandbox`. In Go the constants are `sandbox.LifecycleCapabilityStop` and the rest of that set. A missing operation fails before the provider is contacted. Daytona can still reject a supported call for one sandbox, and that error comes back to you.

The snippets below assume you already have a `client`. Build one on the provider page.

## Open a sandbox

<Tabs groupId="provider" items={['Daytona', 'Modal']} persist>
  <Tab value="Daytona">

`get` loads a sandbox that already exists. `refresh` loads it again.

<Tabs groupId="language" items={['TypeScript', 'Go']} persist>
  <Tab value="TypeScript">

```ts
const sandbox = await client.get(process.env.DAYTONA_SANDBOX_ID!);
const info = await sandbox.refresh();
console.log(sandbox.id, info.state);
```

  </Tab>
  <Tab value="Go">

```go
instance, err := client.Get(ctx, os.Getenv("DAYTONA_SANDBOX_ID"))
if err != nil {
	return err
}
if err := instance.Refresh(ctx); err != nil {
	return err
}
```

`Attach` is not available.

  </Tab>
</Tabs>

  </Tab>
  <Tab value="Modal">

`attach` builds a handle from an id. It does not ask Modal whether that sandbox exists. Modal has no `get` and no `refresh`.

<Tabs groupId="language" items={['TypeScript', 'Go']} persist>
  <Tab value="TypeScript">

```ts
const sandbox = await client.attach(process.env.MODAL_SANDBOX_ID!);
console.log(sandbox.id);
```

  </Tab>
  <Tab value="Go">

```go
instance, err := client.Attach(ctx, os.Getenv("MODAL_SANDBOX_ID"))
if err != nil {
	return err
}
```

  </Tab>
</Tabs>

  </Tab>
</Tabs>

## Stop, start, pause, and archive

These four calls are Daytona calls. Modal rejects them. `stop` asks Daytona to stop the sandbox. The sandbox stays available to start again. `start` brings a stopped or archived sandbox back. `pause` keeps memory and stops the sandbox. `archive` moves a retained sandbox into archive storage.

<Tabs groupId="language" items={['TypeScript', 'Go']} persist>
  <Tab value="TypeScript">

```ts
const sandbox = await client.get(process.env.DAYTONA_SANDBOX_ID!);

if (!sandbox.supports(LifecycleCapability.Stop)) {
  throw new Error(`sandbox ${sandbox.id} cannot be stopped`);
}

const info = await sandbox.stop({ timeout: 60_000 });
console.log(sandbox.id, info.state);
```

`timeout` is milliseconds. Pass `force: true` to request an immediate stop. Check `supports` the same way before `start`, `pause`, or `archive`.

  </Tab>
  <Tab value="Go">

```go
instance, err := client.Get(ctx, os.Getenv("DAYTONA_SANDBOX_ID"))
if err != nil {
	return err
}
if !instance.Supports(sandbox.LifecycleCapabilityStop) {
	return fmt.Errorf("sandbox %s cannot be stopped", instance.ID())
}
if err := instance.Stop(ctx, &sandbox.StopOptions{
	Timeout: sandbox.Duration(time.Minute),
}); err != nil {
	return err
}
```

`Start`, `Pause`, and `Archive` take the same kind of options. Check `Supports` before each one.

  </Tab>
</Tabs>

Daytona decides whether that sandbox can pause, archive, or stop. An ephemeral sandbox can still be removed by the lifetime you set at [create](/docs/create#daytona) time.

## Delete

`delete` removes a Daytona sandbox. Modal rejects it.

<Tabs groupId="language" items={['TypeScript', 'Go']} persist>
  <Tab value="TypeScript">

```ts
const sandbox = await client.get(process.env.DAYTONA_SANDBOX_ID!);
await sandbox.delete({ timeout: 60_000 });
```

  </Tab>
  <Tab value="Go">

```go
instance, err := client.Get(ctx, os.Getenv("DAYTONA_SANDBOX_ID"))
if err != nil {
	return err
}
if err := instance.Delete(ctx, &sandbox.DeleteOptions{
	Timeout: sandbox.Duration(time.Minute),
}); err != nil {
	return err
}
```

  </Tab>
</Tabs>

## Terminate

`terminate` finishes a Modal sandbox. It is not a stop, and it is not a delete. Daytona rejects it. A maximum lifetime or an idle timeout set at [create](/docs/create#modal) time also ends the sandbox.

<Tabs groupId="language" items={['TypeScript', 'Go']} persist>
  <Tab value="TypeScript">

```ts
const sandbox = await client.attach(process.env.MODAL_SANDBOX_ID!);

if (!sandbox.supports(LifecycleCapability.Terminate)) {
  throw new Error(`sandbox ${sandbox.id} cannot be terminated`);
}

const exit = await sandbox.terminate({ wait: true, timeout: 60_000 });
console.log(sandbox.id, exit.exitCode);
```

`wait: true` polls until the sandbox exits or the timeout fires. The timeout is how long the client waits. Modal does not receive that duration as its own terminate timeout.

  </Tab>
  <Tab value="Go">

```go
instance, err := client.Attach(ctx, os.Getenv("MODAL_SANDBOX_ID"))
if err != nil {
	return err
}
if !instance.Supports(sandbox.LifecycleCapabilityTerminate) {
	return fmt.Errorf("sandbox %s cannot be terminated", instance.ID())
}
exit, err := instance.Terminate(ctx, &sandbox.TerminateOptions{
	Wait:    sandbox.Value(true),
	Timeout: sandbox.Duration(time.Minute),
})
if err != nil {
	return err
}
fmt.Println(exit.GetExitCode())
```

